date-released: 2026-05-09
repository-code: "https://github.com/jespergran98/prime-line-cover"
license: MIT
abstract: >
  An exact branch-and-bound solver for the minimum line cover of prime-indexed
  points (OEIS A373813). Given N prime points (k, p_k) in the plane, the solver
  certifies f(N) — the minimum number of straight lines needed to cover all N
  points — for N = 1 through 1024. It extends the prior certified boundary from
  N = 861 — reached in approximately 282 hours via a general-purpose MIP solver —
  to N = 1024, with f(1024) = 143, in under 40 hours on a Google Cloud
  c4d-highcpu-8 instance (8 vCPUs, 15 GB RAM). At startup, all 12,162 heavy
  lines (those through at least three prime points) are enumerated and stored as
  1024-bit bitmasks; the incremental sweep then carries forward a primal
  warm-start solution, a Lagrangian dual seed, and a witness cover from f(N-1)
  at each step. Optimality is certified per step via witness propagation (mode W),
  root-level bound closure (mode R), or full parallel branch-and-bound (mode D).
  Lower bounds combine a cover-inequality floor and a Lagrangian gain bound
  tightened by projected subgradient descent with coordinate-descent polishing.
  The Exclusive Dependency Rule forces productive heavy lines into the partial
  solution without loss of optimality. Strong branching evaluates a shortlist of
  candidate lines via Lagrangian simulation; independent subproblems are
  dispatched to worker threads via a shared atomic task queue. The 163-term
  extension identifies 20 new awkward primes (N = 864 through 1015, cover sizes
  124 to 143). Every reported value is certified optimal; the solver has no
  heuristic stopping rule.
keywords:
  - prime points
  - minimum line cover
  - parallel search
  - branch and bound
  - Lagrangian relaxation
  - cover inequalities
  - witness propagation
  - incremental sweep
  - awkward primes
  - OEIS A373813
